    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Suppose $x,y\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-1\\}$. Prove that\n$$\\frac{x+1}{y+1}=1$$\nif and only if $x=y$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Multiplying by $y+1\\ne0$ gives $x+1=y+1$, hence $x=y$. Conversely, $x=y$ gives $\\frac{x+1}{y+1}=1$.",
          "worked": "Forward direction: Suppose $\\frac{x+1}{y+1}=1$. Since $y+1\\ne0$, multiplying by $y+1$ gives $x+1=y+1$, so $x=y$.\n\nReverse direction: Suppose $x=y$. Since $y\\ne-1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x+1}{y+1}&=\\frac{y+1}{y+1}\\\\\\frac{x+1}{y+1}&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth directions hold, proving the equivalence.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose $m,n\\in\\mathbb N$. Prove that\n$$m^3+mn^2=m^2n+n^3$$\nif and only if $m=n$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Factor to obtain $(m-n)(m^2+n^2)=0$, which gives $m=n$. Conversely, substituting $m=n$ makes both sides $2m^3$.",
          "worked": "Forward direction: Suppose $m^3+mn^2=m^2n+n^3$. Rearranging and factoring gives\n$$\\begin{align*}m^3+mn^2-m^2n-n^3&=0\\\\(m-n)(m^2+n^2)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nIf $m^2+n^2>0$, then $m-n=0$, so $m=n$. If $m^2+n^2=0$, then $m=n=0$, which also gives $m=n$.\n\nReverse direction: Suppose $m=n$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}m^3+mn^2&=m^3+m^3\\\\m^3+mn^2&=2m^3,\\end{align*}$$\nand\n$$\\begin{align*}m^2n+n^3&=m^3+m^3\\\\m^2n+n^3&=2m^3.\\end{align*}$$\nThus both directions hold.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Suppose $a,b\\in\\mathbb Z$. Prove that $a^2-b^2$ is odd if and only if $a-b$ is odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a^2-b^2=(a+b)(a-b)$. An odd product has odd factors. Conversely, if $a-b$ is odd, then $a+b=(a-b)+2b$ is also odd, so their product is odd.",
          "worked": "Forward direction: Suppose $a^2-b^2$ is odd. Since\n$$a^2-b^2=(a+b)(a-b),$$\nboth integer factors must be odd; otherwise their product would be even. In particular, $a-b$ is odd.\n\nReverse direction: Suppose $a-b$ is odd. Write $a-b=2k+1$ for some $k\\in\\mathbb Z$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}a+b&=(a-b)+2b\\\\a+b&=2k+1+2b\\\\a+b&=2(k+b)+1.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $a+b$ and $a-b$ are both odd. Their product $a^2-b^2$ is therefore odd. Both directions hold.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q10-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Suppose $m,n\\in\\mathbb Z$. Prove that $m^3-n^3$ is even if and only if $m-n$ is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "An even difference of cubes means the cubes, and hence $m,n$, have the same parity. Conversely, $m^3-n^3=(m-n)(m^2+mn+n^2)$ is even when $m-n$ is even.",
          "worked": "Forward direction: Suppose $m^3-n^3$ is even. Then $m^3$ and $n^3$ have the same parity. An integer and its cube have the same parity, so $m$ and $n$ are both even or both odd. Hence $m-n$ is even.\n\nReverse direction: Suppose $m-n$ is even. Write $m-n=2k$ for some $k\\in\\mathbb Z$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}m^3-n^3&=(m-n)(m^2+mn+n^2)\\\\m^3-n^3&=2k(m^2+mn+n^2)\\\\m^3-n^3&=2\\bigl[k(m^2+mn+n^2)\\bigr].\\end{align*}$$\nThe expression in brackets is an integer, so $m^3-n^3$ is even. Both directions hold.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      }
    ]
  },
